# Incident Simulation: Linux Attack Chain with Wazuh Detection

A writeup from the same SOC home lab, covering the second monitored endpoint — a Linux host. It documents three attack techniques against Rocky Linux, their detection through `auditd`, `/var/log/secure`, and File Integrity Monitoring, a custom detection rule, and a full root-cause analysis of a real telemetry-delivery problem. Every technique is mapped to MITRE ATT&CK.

> **Nature of this material:** a controlled simulation in an isolated lab. All actions were performed manually on a dedicated test machine using benign commands. The objective was to practise detection, analysis, and troubleshooting on Linux telemetry.

---

## 1. Lab environment

| Component | Details |
|---|---|
| SIEM/XDR | Wazuh 4.14.7 (same all-in-one server as the Windows lab) |
| Server | Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS, 192.168.122.125 |
| Monitored endpoint | Rocky Linux 9 (Minimal) |
| Hostname / agent | rocky-agent / agent id 002 |
| Endpoint telemetry | `auditd` (command execution), File Integrity Monitoring (syscheck), `/var/log/secure` |
| Virtualisation | QEMU/KVM, isolated libvirt NAT 192.168.122.0/24 |

**Telemetry configured for visibility:**
- `auditd` installed and enabled, with custom watch rules on cron files and `execve` for selected binaries
- FIM in real-time mode on `/etc`, `/usr/bin`, `/usr/sbin`, `/root`, `/home`, `/tmp`
- Agent collecting `/var/log/secure` (authentication, sudo) and `/var/log/audit/audit.log`

**Timeline note:** the Wazuh server runs in UTC while the Rocky host displays local time (CEST, +02:00). Event times should be read from the dashboard in UTC for consistency across sources — a practical reminder that mixed time zones make raw-log correlation error-prone (see Analyst findings).

