## 4. Custom detection rule (Linux)

The second custom rule of the lab, mapped to T1059.004. It raises audited executions carrying the `suspicious_exec` key from the silent level 0 to an actionable alert.

```xml
<group name="audit,attack,custom,">
  <rule id="100200" level="10">
    <if_sid>80700</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.key">suspicious_exec</field>
    <description>Suspicious tool execution on Linux via audited binary (key: $(audit.key)) - MITRE T1059.004</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>
</group>
```

**Logic:** builds on rule 80700 (the grouped-auditd base), fires only when the event carries the `suspicious_exec` audit key, raises it to level 10, and maps it to T1059.004.

**Verification:** validated with `wazuh-logtest` against a real `SYSCALL` line — a key detail, because auditd events span multiple lines (SYSCALL carries the key, EXECVE carries the arguments), and the rule condition (`audit.key`) is only present on the SYSCALL line. Testing the wrong line returns level 0 and looks like a failure when the rule is in fact correct.

