## 5. Operational finding: auditd events not reaching the dashboard (root-cause analysis)

The most instructive part of the Linux lab was a real telemetry-delivery failure: rule 100200 fired correctly in `wazuh-logtest`, `auditd` recorded the event locally (`ausearch` showed it), yet no alert appeared in the dashboard on live traffic. This was diagnosed by eliminating layers from source to destination:

| Layer checked | Tool | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Detection rule | `wazuh-logtest` | Rule 100200 fires (level 10, T1059.004) — rule is correct |
| Duplicate agent IDs | `manage_agents -l` | Clean — 001 windows, 002 rocky — not the cause |
| Server resources | `free -h` | ~5 GB available — not the cause |
| Clock sync | `timedatectl` | Both hosts in sync (2 s apart in UTC) — not the cause; the apparent 2 h gap was a UTC-vs-CEST display artefact |
| Agent connectivity | agent `ossec.log` | Flapping "Lost connection" — traced to **our own manager restarts** during rule debugging, not a persistent fault |
| Event in the monitored file | `grep audit.log` | **Empty** — the event was not in the file Wazuh reads |
| auditd write config | `/etc/audit/auditd.conf` | `log_format = ENRICHED` — **root cause** |

**Root cause:** `auditd` was writing in **ENRICHED** format. `ausearch` reads events through auditd's own interface (so it saw them), but the Wazuh decoder reads the plaintext `audit.log` and did not parse the ENRICHED entries — so from the SIEM's perspective the events did not exist. This is a common Rocky/RHEL 9 default.

**Remediation attempted:** switch `log_format` to `RAW` and restart auditd (note: on RHEL, auditd is restarted with `service auditd restart`, not `systemctl`). At the time of writing, live delivery of `execve` events remained an open issue on this host, tracked for follow-up; local detection and rule logic are fully proven.

**Why this matters:** "data not arriving" in a SIEM is almost never a single obvious cause. The correct response is layered elimination from source to destination — exactly the process above. The lesson: a rule that works in a test harness can still be invisible in production if the transport or the source format is wrong, and the analyst must be able to tell those apart.

